<h2>Why the legislature closed the door</h2>
<p>Matrimonial adjudication depends on candour about the most private aspects of life. A spouse deterred from testifying truthfully by the presence of onlookers — or by the fear of newspaper reproduction — cannot give the court the material it needs. The in-camera provisions therefore serve the trial&rsquo;s integrity as much as the parties&rsquo; dignity: they trade public spectacle for honest evidence. The publication bar completes the design by ensuring that what is said behind the closed door stays out of print.</p>

<h2>The two provisions, side by side</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Section 22, Hindu Marriage Act</th><th>Section 11, Family Courts Act</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Trigger</td><td>Either party&rsquo;s desire, or the court&rsquo;s discretion</td><td>Either party&rsquo;s desire (mandatory), or the Family Court&rsquo;s own motion</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Scope</td><td>Proceedings under the HMA</td><td>All proceedings before a Family Court, across personal laws</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Publication</td><td>Barred; HC/SC judgments publishable only with the court&rsquo;s prior permission; breach punishable with fine</td><td>No separate publication clause — Section 22 HMA or general law supplies it where applicable</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>What &ldquo;in camera&rdquo; changes — and what it does not</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Changes:</strong> the public and press are excluded during the hearing; depositions on intimate matters are recorded without an audience; the psychological pressure on parties and witnesses drops measurably.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Does not change:</strong> the opposing party&rsquo;s full participation and right of cross-examination; the court record itself, which remains complete; the appellate court&rsquo;s access to everything; and certified-copy rights of the parties.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Judgments:</strong> trial court judgments in HMA matters fall within the publication bar; High Court and Supreme Court judgments can be published only with prior permission — which modern courts often effectively grant through anonymised neutral-citation publication.</div>
</div>

<h2>Strategic considerations</h2>
<p>For the party pleading sensitive facts — sexual conduct, medical or psychiatric history, allegations touching children — an in-camera order should be sought at the first effective hearing, so that every deposition is taken under its cover. For respondents facing lurid but weak allegations, the calculus is identical from the other direction: privacy prevents the pleadings from being weaponised through circulation in the family or community. Where one spouse appears to be feeding proceedings to the press or to relatives on social media, counsel should seek a specific restraint order referencing Section 22(1), with its penal consequence, rather than relying on general appeals to decency. In children&rsquo;s matters, courts add their own protective layers, and counsel should propose anonymisation of cause titles where the facts warrant.</p>

<h2>A short practice checklist</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Raise the in-camera request at the earliest hearing and have it recorded in the order sheet; protection that begins mid-trial cannot recall evidence already given in open court.</p>
  <p>Pair the request with a publication-restraint direction when there is any history of leaks, and serve it on the parties personally.</p>
  <p>Remember the bar binds parties too: circulating the other side&rsquo;s pleadings on WhatsApp groups sits squarely within the mischief Section 22 penalises.</p>
  <p>When citing matrimonial judgments in other cases, use anonymised or officially published versions, and seek permission where the source is an unreported HMA matter.</p>
</div>
